mikolai feels like a city with its fate  hanging over it  russian forces are about 20 kilometers  to the east now  there is a sense of inevitability about  what's coming next  some neighborhoods are almost deserted  stray dogs and cats now wander through  the abandoned homes  fuel is running out most petrol stations  are now completely empty  the trains stopped running some time ago  the railway station is now a military  outpost  dimitro plattenchuk shows us what  remains of mykolive's city hall it was  hit by a cruise missile just as everyone  was arriving to work one morning was  anyone in it at the time  such a six pill that people died  36.  missile came through on the fourth floor  where the governor had his office but  barely an inch has escaped damage  dimitro's grandfather defended mikolai  from the germans in world war ii he and  his brother are now doing the same 78  years later this time though against the  russians when was the last time you had  a day off  24  of february  three months ago every day you've been  working defending your country yeah from  5 00 a.m to  10 p.m  you must be tired  work because  it's our job  now  there is no running water in nikolai  so twice a day trucks with bowsers  circle the city for people to stock up  it was here we met lude miller filling  up plastic bottles for washing and  cooking  i'm 83 years old and my sister is 81 and  very sick she tells me as we help carry  her bottles the roads are being shelled  and my sister can't walk downstairs  we've had to stay here we will stay we  are old and we can't go we love our  homeland we are ukrainians  ludmilla lives on the third floor it is  a struggle for her to get up the stairs  and to be frank she has little chance of  getting down to the bunker when the  missiles strike in the middle of the  night  she shares the tiny apartment just two  rooms with her younger sister i asked  her what she thinks of vladimir putin  he's sick in the head she says can't  someone just kill him can't they remove  the sick old man  the two sisters will stay here together  they know they very probably might die  here together but whatever happens they  won't leave mikolav they only have each  other  is that determination and loyalty in a  way  is what russia is up against  alice debunker sky news in mikolai of  eastern ukraine
 

